hat can be covered **without using sunny lines** is at most \\(n\\).  \n> Since \\(|T_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}>n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), this is impossible.  \n> Hence at least two of \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\) are non\u2011empty.\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error:**  \n* The statement \u201ca line of a fixed direction can contain at most one point from each of the \\(n\\) columns (or rows)\u201d is true, but the conclusion \u201cthe total number of points \u2026 is at most \\(n\\)\u201d is false.  \n  * Example: a single horizontal line \\(y=1\\) contains **\\(n\\)** points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots,(n,1)\\) of \\(T_n\\).  \n  * With two horizontal lines we could already cover up to \\(2n\\) points, etc.  \nThus the bound \\(n\\) severely underestimates what can be covered by non\u2011sunny lines, and the argument that \u201cat most two families non\u2011empty\u201d leads to a contradiction is invalid.\n\nBecause this claim is used to deduce that at least two families are non\u2011empty, the deduction is not justified. However, the later part of the proof *does* assume at least two families are non\u2011empty, which may still be true for other reasons, but the justification given is incorrect.\n\n---\n\n### Paragraph 4 \u2013 Assuming \\(\\mathcal H\\neq\\varnothing\\) and \\(\\mathcal V\\neq\\varnothing\\)  \n\n> \u201cAssume \\(\\mathcal H\\neq\\varnothing\\) and \\(\\mathcal V\\neq\\varnothing\\); let \\(h=|\\mathcal H|\\) and \\(v=|\\mathcal V|\\).  \n> Points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) are covered by the diagonal lines of \\(\\mathcal D\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error:**  \nThe solution **assumes** without proof that the diagonal lines \\(\\mathcal D\\) cover *all* points on the line \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \n* It is possible that \\(|\\mathcal D|<n\\) (since there are \\(n\\) points on that diagonal) and that some of those points are instead covered by a horizontal or vertical line.  \n* No argument is provided that every point with \\(a+b=n+1\\) must be on a line of slope \\(-1\\). Hence the statement is unjustified.\n\n---\n\n### Paragraph 5 \u2013 Claim about points with \\(a+b<n+1\\)  \n\n> \u201cFor a point with \\(a+b<n+1\\) the equation \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\le n\\) defines a line of slope \\(-1\\) that meets the triangle \\(T_n\\) in **at most one lattice point**, so such a point cannot be covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal D\\).  \n> Consequently every point of  \n> \\[\n> U_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b<n+1\\}\n> \\]\n> must lie on a line of \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error:**  \n* The fact that a line \\(x+y=c\\) meets the triangle in at most one lattice point is correct.  \n* However, the conclusion that **no** point of \\(U_n\\) can be covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal D\\) is false: a diagonal line of slope \\(-1\\) can certainly pass through a point of \\(U_n\\); it simply cannot pass through two distinct points of \\(U_n\\).  \n* Therefore a point of \\(U_n\\) *may* be covered by a diagonal line; the solution ignores this possibility.  \n* Consequently the statement \u201cevery point of \\(U_n\\) must lie on a line of \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\)\u201d is unjustified.\n\n---\n\n### Paragraph 6 \u2013 Deriving inequality (1)  \n\n> \u201cSince each horizontal line can intersect each vertical line in at most one lattice point, the total number of points of \\(U_n\\) that can be covered by \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\) does not exceed \\(hv\\).  Therefore  \n> \\[\n> hv\\ge |U_n|=\\binom{n}{2}. \\tag{1}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error:**  \n* The inequality \\(hv\\ge|U_n|\\) follows from the previous (incorrect) claim that **all** points of \\(U_n\\) must be covered by a horizontal\u2011vertical pair.  \n* In reality, each diagonal line could cover one point of \\(U_n\\); the correct necessary condition would be  \n  \\[\n  hv+|\\mathcal D|\\ge |U_n|.\n  \\]  \n* Because the argument omitted the contribution of \\(\\mathcal D\\), inequality (1) is not justified and is generally false. For example, with \\(n=4\\), \\(U_4\\) has \\(\\binom{4}{2}=6\\) points, but one can cover them with \\(h=2, v=2\\) (giving \\(hv=4\\)) plus a single diagonal line covering the remaining two points.\n\nThus (1) is a **critical error** that invalidates the subsequent bound.\n\n---\n\n### Paragraph 7 \u2013 Upper bound on \\(hv\\)  \n\n> \u201cBecause \\(h+v\\le n-k\\), the product \\(hv\\) is maximised when \\(h\\) and \\(v\\) are as equal as possible, giving  \n> \\[\n> hv\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n-k)^2}{4}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{2}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification:** The inequality (2) is a standard consequence of the arithmetic\u2011geometric mean inequality; it is correct **provided** the variables \\(h\\) and \\(v\\) are non\u2011negative integers with sum at most \\(n-k\\). No issue here.\n\n---\n\n### Paragraph 8 \u2013 Combining (1) and (2)  \n\n> \u201cCombining (1) and (2) we obtain  \n> \\[\n> \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n-k)^2}{4}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n> \\]  \n> A direct verification (consider separately the parity of \\(n-k\\)) shows that this inequality forces  \n> \\[\n> k\\le n-2 .\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error:**  \nBecause inequality (1) is false, the combined inequality is baseless. Even if (1) were true, the subsequent algebraic verification is omitted; however the main problem is that the premise is invalid, so the derived bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) is **not proven**.\n\n---\n\n### Paragraph 9 \u2013 Configurations with \\(k=0\\)  \n\n> \u201c*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \n> Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n> \\[\n> L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n> \\]  \n> For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  All lines are non\u2011sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realised.\u201d\n\n**Verification:** This construction indeed covers every point of \\(T_n\\) (each point has its \\(x\\)-coordinate equal to some \\(i\\) between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\)). The lines are vertical, thus non\u2011sunny. No issue.\n\n---\n\n### Paragraph 10 \u2013 Configurations with \\(k=1\\) (first attempt)  \n\n> \u201c*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \n> Begin with the family of vertical lines above and replace the line \\(x=n\\) by the diagonal line  \n> \\[\n> D:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n> \\]  \n> Now the family consists of \\(n-1\\) vertical lines (non\u2011sunny) and one diagonal line (also non\u2011sunny), still giving \\(k=0\\).\u201d\n\n**Verification:** The diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) indeed has slope \\(-1\\), which is **non\u2011sunny**. The family now has \\(n\\) lines, all non\u2011sunny, so the configuration still yields \\(k=0\\). This intermediate step is correct.\n\n---\n\n### Paragraph 11 \u2013 Configurations with \\(k=1\\) (final step)  \n\n> \u201cTo obtain exactly one sunny line, modify the line \\(x=1\\) as follows: choose a slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\) and consider the line  \n> \\[\n> S:\\;y=m(x-1)+1 .\n> \\]  \n> The line \\(S\\) passes through the point \\((1,1)\\); consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) remains covered.  All other points are covered exactly as before (by a vertical line \\(x=a\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) or by the diagonal \\(D\\)).  Hence we obtain a configuration with precisely one sunny line.\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error:**  \n* After replacing the vertical line \\(x=1\\) with the sunny line \\(S\\), the points \\((1,b)\\) for \\(b=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) are **no longer covered**.  \n  * The line \\(S\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) but, unless \\(m=0\\) (which is prohibited), it does **not** pass through any other point with \\(x=1\\).  \n* The claim \u201cevery point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) remains covered\u201d is false. Consequently the configuration fails to satisfy the covering requirement, and the construction for \\(k=1\\) is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### Paragraph 12 \u2013 Conclusion  \n\n> \u201cWe have proved that any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) can contain at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n> We have also exhibited explicit constructions for the two extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).  \n> The existence of configurations for the intermediate values \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\) remains an open problem.  \n> \\[\n> \\boxed{\\text{All that is known rigorously: }k\\le n-2,\\;k=0,1\\text{ are attainable.}}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Issues \u2013 Critical Errors:**  \n* The upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) is not rigorously established (see errors in Paragraphs\u202f3\u20118).  \n* The construction for \\(k=1\\) is incorrect (Paragraph\u202f11).  \n* The problem asks for **all** admissible \\(k\\); leaving the intermediate values unresolved means the solution does not answer the question.  \n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment  \n\nThe solution contains multiple critical logical and factual mistakes:\n\n1. An incorrect bound on the number of points that can be covered by non\u2011sunny lines (Paragraph\u202f3).  \n2. Unjustified assumptions that diagonal lines must cover the entire boundary \\(a+b=n+1\\) and cannot cover any interior point (Paragraph\u202f4\u20115).  \n3. Derivation of the key inequality \\(hv\\ge\\binom{n}{2}\\) without accounting for the possible contribution of diagonal lines (Paragraph\u202f6).  \n4. A flawed construction for \\(k=1\\) that leaves many required points uncovered (Paragraph\u202f11).  \n\nBecause these errors invalidate both the claimed upper bound and the existence proof for \\(k=1\\), the solution does **not** correctly solve the problem."

