            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) contains \\(\\displaystyle\\binom{n+1}{2}\\) points.  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=-x+(i+1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*The slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(-1\\); therefore \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and is **not sunny**.*\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1 (Covering by the \\(L_i\\)).  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) there exists a unique integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) such that \\(a+b=i+1\\). Then  \n\n\\[\nb = -a + (i+1),\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) satisfies equation (1) for that \\(i\\). ∎\n\n\n\nNow for each \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nM_i:\\; y=\\frac12 x+\\frac{i+1}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(M_i\\) is **sunny**.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2 (Diagonal preservation).  \nFor a fixed \\(i\\) let  \n\n\\[\nD_i=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=i+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen every point of \\(D_i\\) satisfies equation (2); i.e. \\(D_i\\subseteq M_i\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in D_i\\) then \\(a+b=i+1\\). Compute  \n\n\\[\n\\frac12 a+\\frac{i+1}{2}