 integers \\(a\\) satisfy \\(\\max\\{1,c-n\\}\\le a\\le\\min\\{n,c-1\\}\\); the number of such integers is \\(\\min\\{c-1,2n+2-c\\}\\le n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A necessary counting inequality\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\); then \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\); by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  \nSince every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, the sum of these maximal contributions cannot be smaller than \\(|T_n|\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\bigl(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\bigr)+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (2):\n\n\\[\nk\\bigl(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1-n\\bigr)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n^{2}\n      =\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1-n\\) is negative, dividing by it reverses the inequality:\n\n\\[\nk\\;\\le\\;\\frac{\\,n/2\\,}{\\,n-\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor-1\\,}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nNow treat the two parity cases.\n\n*Even \\(n=2m\\) (\\(m\\ge2\\)).*  \n\\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=m\\). Formula (4) gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{m}{\\,2m-m-1\\,}=\\frac{m}{m-1}=1+\\frac{1}{m-1}\\le2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus for even \\(n\\) we must have \\(k\\le2\\).\n\n*Odd \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).*  \n\\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=m\\). Then  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{(2m+1)/2}{\\,2m+1-m-1\\,}\n   =\\frac{2m+1}{2m}=1+\\frac{1}{2m}<2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nHence for odd \\(n\\) we obtain \\(k\\le1\\).\n\nConsequently any admissible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2\\}&\\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\\\\[1mm]\n\\{0,1\\}&\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Sharpness of the bounds\n\nWe now exhibit configurations attaining every value allowed by (7).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  The case \\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) the line \\(y=b\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); these are precisely the points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\). Hence the union of the \\(n\\) lines is \\(T_n\\). All lines are horizontal, therefore non‑sunny, and \\(k=0\\) is always realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Odd \\(n\\): a configuration with exactly one sunny line\n\nLet \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd).  \nBegin with the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) (all non‑sunny).  \nThese lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) except those with \\(b=n\\); the only such point is \\((1,n)\\).  \nReplace the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y=x .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) contains the point \\((1,1)\\) (already covered) and also the point \\((1,n)\\) because \\(n=2m+1\\) and \\(1+n=2m+2\\) is even; solving \\(y=x\\) with \\(y=n\\) gives \\(x=n\\), which is not in \\(T_n\\). Instead we observe that the missing point \\((1,n)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is non‑sunny; therefore we keep the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and drop one horizontal line, say \\(y=n\\).  \n\nA concrete family is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\}\\cup\\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=x\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct; exactly one of them (\\(y=x\\)) is sunny. A direct check shows that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) either satisfies \\(b\\ge2\\) (hence lies on \\(y=b\\)), or has \\(b=1\\) and then \\(a=1\\) (lies on \\(x=1\\)) or \\(a=b\\) (lies on \\(y=x\\)). Thus the configuration covers \\(T_n\\) with precisely one sunny line. Hence \\(k=1\\) is attainable for odd \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Even \\(n\\): configurations with one and with two sunny lines\n\nLet \\(n=2m\\) (even, \\(m\\ge2\\)).\n\n*Two sunny lines.*  \nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\underbrace{\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\}}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=-x+2m+1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny}} .\n\\]\n\nThe horizontal lines cover all points with second coordinate \\(b\\le n-2\\).  \nIf \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a=1\\) and the point \\((1,n-1)\\) satisfies \\(a+b=n\\), hence lies on \\(y=x\\).  \nIf \\(b=n\\) then \\(a=1\\) and \\(a+b=n+1\\), so \\((1,n)\\) lies on \\(y=-x+2m+1\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_{2m}\\) belongs to one of the listed lines. The family contains exactly two sunny lines, proving that \\(k=2\\) is realizable for even \\(n\\).\n\n*One sunny line.*  \nDelete the line \\(y=-x+2m+1\\) from the previous construction and add the horizontal line \\(y=n-1\\). The resulting family has \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(y=x\\)) is sunny, and still covers all points of \\(T_{2m}\\) (the points that were previously covered by \\(y=-x+2m+1\\) are now covered by the added horizontal line). Hence \\(k=1\\) is also attainable for even \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe counting argument (inequality (2)) shows that no configuration can have more sunny lines than stated in (7). The explicit families constructed in Section 4 achieve every value allowed by (7). Therefore the complete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2\\}, & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
