            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The set of required points  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for \\(n\\) distinct lines whose union contains every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nCounting the points:\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{b=1}^{n}(n+1-b)=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  How many points can a single line contain?\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) in the integer form  \n\n\\[\nqx-py=c,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(p,q\\) are coprime integers, \\(p\\neq0\\), \\(q\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\ell\\cap T_n\\,|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) are two distinct lattice points on \\(\\ell\\), the vector \\((a'-a,b'-b)\\) is an integer multiple of \\((p,q)\\). Consequently the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by \\(|p|+|q|\\) when we move from one lattice point of \\(\\ell\\) to the next.  \n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Let \\((a_0,b_0)\\) be a point of \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\) with minimal value of \\(a+b\\). The next lattice point on \\(\\ell\\) has sum \\((a_0+b_0)+(|p|+|q|)\\), the following one \\((a_0+b_0)+2(|p|+|q|)\\), and so on. As soon as the sum exceeds \\(n+1\\) we leave \\(T_n\\). Hence the number of lattice points of \\(\\ell\\) that belong to \\(T_n\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-(a_0+b_0)}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|p|,|q|\\ge1\\) and \\((p,q)\\neq(1,-1)\\), we have \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\), which yields (2). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (non‑sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be horizontal (\\(y=b\\)), vertical (\\(x=a\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\ell\\cap T_n\\,|\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Horizontal.*  For \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); thus the line contains \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Vertical.*  By symmetry the same bound holds.\n\n*Slope \\(-1\\).*  Such a line is \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nThe admissible integers \\(a\\) satisfy \\(\\max\\{1,c-n\\}\\le a\\le\\min\\{n,c-1\\}\\); the number of such integers is \\(\\min\\{c-1,2n+2-c\\}\\le n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A necessary counting inequality\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\); then \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\); by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  \nSince every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, the sum of these maximal contributions cannot be smaller than \\(|T_n|\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\bigl(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\bigr)+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (4) gives\n\n\\[\nk\\;\\le\\;\n\\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n2 &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy the bound (5).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Sharpness – explicit constructions\n\nWe now exhibit, for every \\(n\\), families of \\(n\\) distinct lines attaining each value of \\(k\\) allowed by (5).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  \\(k=0\\) for all \\(n\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_n=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(b\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\)) the line \\(y=b\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); these are precisely the points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\). Hence \\(\\bigcup\\mathcal H_n=T_n\\). All lines are horizontal, therefore non‑sunny, and \\(k=0\\) is realized for every \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Odd \\(n\\): a configuration with exactly one sunny line\n\nLet \\(n\\) be odd and set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{\\text{odd}}_1=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\ell}_{\\text{sunny}},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell\\) is the line through the two points  \n\n\\[\n(1,n)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad(2,n-2).\n\\]\n\nIts equation is \\(y-n=-2(x-1)\\); the slope \\(-2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.\n\n*Coverage check.*  \nThe only point of \\(T_n\\) with second coordinate \\(b=n\\) is \\((1,n)\\); it lies on \\(\\ell\\).  \nAll remaining points have \\(b\\le n-1\\) and therefore belong to the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) that is present in the family. Consequently every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) is on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L^{\\text{odd}}_1\\). The family contains exactly one sunny line, so \\(k=1\\) is attainable for odd \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Even \\(n=2m\\): configurations with two and with one sunny line\n\n**Two sunny lines.**  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{\\text{even}}_2=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2\\,\\}}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\ell_{1}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\ell_{2}}_{\\text{sunny}},\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:\\;y-n=-2(x-1),\\qquad\n\\ell_{2}:\\;y-(n-1)=-2(x-1).\n\\]\n\nBoth lines have slope \\(-2\\) and are therefore sunny; they are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n*Coverage check.*  \nPoints with \\(b\\le n-2\\) lie on the corresponding horizontal line.  \nThe remaining points of \\(T_n\\) are:\n\\[\n(1,n),\\;(2,n-2)\\in\\ell_{1},\\qquad\n(1,n-1),\\;(2,n-3)\\in\\ell_{2}.\n\\]\nNo other point of \\(T_n\\) has \\(b=n-1\\) or \\(b=n\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L^{\\text{even}}_2\\). The family contains exactly two sunny lines, proving that \\(k=2\\) is possible for even \\(n\\).\n\n**One sunny line.**  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) and add the sunny line \\(\\ell\\) used in the odd case, i.e. the line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,n-2)\\). Explicitly,\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{\\text{even}}_1=\n\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\ell\\},\n\\qquad\n\\ell:\\;y-n=-2(x-1).\n\\]\n\nAll points with \\(b\\le n-1\\) are on a horizontal line; the unique point with \\(b=n\\) is \\((1,n)\\) and lies on \\(\\ell\\). Thus \\(\\mathcal L^{\\text{even}}_1\\) covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly one sunny line. Hence \\(k=1\\) is also attainable for even \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nInequality (5) shows that no configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines can have more sunny lines than stated. The explicit families constructed in Sections 4.1–4.3 achieve every value allowed by (5). Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2\\}, & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
