T_n\\) because \\(a+b\\) is already minimal. Consequently the lattice points of \\(\\ell\\) that belong to \\(T_n\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n(a_0,b_0),\\;(a_0,b_0)+(p,q),\\;(a_0,b_0)+2(p,q),\\dots ,\n\\]\n\nuntil the sum exceeds the maximal allowed value \\(n+1\\).  \nThus the number of such points is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-(a_0+b_0)}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|p|,|q|\\ge1\\) and \\((p,q)\\neq(1,-1)\\), we have \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\), which yields the second inequality in (2). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (non‑sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be horizontal (\\(y=b\\)), vertical (\\(x=a\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\ell\\cap T_n\\,|\\le n .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Horizontal.*  For a fixed \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); hence the line \\(y=b\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Vertical.*  By symmetry the same bound holds.\n\n*Slope \\(-1\\).*  Such a line is \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nThe admissible integer pairs \\((a,b)\\) satisfy \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b=c\\); therefore the number of solutions is exactly \\(c-1\\). Since \\(c\\le n+1\\), we have \\(c-1\\le n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A necessary counting inequality\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\); then \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\); by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  \nSince every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, the sum of these maximal contributions cannot be smaller than \\(|T_n|\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\bigl(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\bigr)+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (4):\n\n\\[\nk\\bigl(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1-n\\bigr)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n^{2}\n      =\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSet \\(m=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\). Then \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1-n = m+1-n = -(n-m-1)\\). Multiplying the inequality by \\(-1\\) (which reverses the sign) gives\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n/2}{\\,n-m-1\\,}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nNow distinguish parity.\n\n*If \\(n=2t+1\\) (odd), then \\(m=t\\) and \\(n-m-1 = (2t+1)-t-1 = t\\).  \nThus (5) yields \\(k\\le\\frac{(2t+1)/2}{t}=1+\\frac{1}{2t}\\).  \nSince \\(k\\) is an integer, \\(k\\le1\\).*\n\n*If \\(n=2t\\) (even, \\(t\\ge2\\) because \\(n\\ge3\\)), then \\(m=t\\) and \\(n-m-1 = 2t-t-1 = t-1\\).  \nHence \\(k\\le\\frac{t}{t-1}=1+\\frac{1}{t-1}\\), which forces \\(k\\le2\\).*\n\nTherefore any admissible configuration must satisfy\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n1 & (n\\text{ odd}),\\\\\n2 & (n\\text{ even}),\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the bound claimed in (†).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Sharpness – explicit constructions\n\nWe now exhibit, for every \\(n\\), families of \\(n\\) distinct lines attaining each value of \\(k\\) allowed by (6).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  \\(k=0\\) for all \\(n\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_n=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(b\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\)) the line \\(y=b\\) contains precisely the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); these are exactly the points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\). Hence \\(\\bigcup\\mathcal H_n=T_n\\). All lines are horizontal, so \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Odd \\(n\\): a configuration with exactly one sunny line\n\nLet \\(n\\) be odd. Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{\\text{odd}}_1=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\ell}_{\\text{sunny}},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell\\) is the line through the two points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,n-2)\\).  \nIts equation is \\(y-n=-2(x-1)\\); the slope \\(-2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nThe only point of \\(T_n\\) with second coordinate \\(b=n\\) is \\((1,n)\\), which lies on \\(\\ell\\).  \nAll other points satisfy \\(b\\le n-1\\) and therefore belong to the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) that is present in the family. Consequently every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) is covered, and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Even \\(n=2m\\): configurations with two and with one sunny line\n\n**Two sunny lines.**  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{\\text{even}}_2=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2\\,\\}}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\ell_{1}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\ell_{2}}_{\\text{sunny}},\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:\\;y-n=-2(x-1),\\qquad\n\\ell_{2}:\\;y-(n-1)=-2(x-1).\n\\]\n\nBoth have slope \\(-2\\) and are distinct because their intercepts differ.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nPoints with \\(b\\le n-2\\) lie on the corresponding horizontal line.  \nThe remaining points of \\(T_n\\) are:\n\\[\n(1,n),\\;(2,n-2)\\in\\ell_{1},\\qquad\n(1,n-1),\\;(2,n-3)\\in\\ell_{2}.\n\\]\nNo other point of \\(T_n\\) has \\(b=n\\) or \\(b=n-1\\); indeed, the definition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a=1\\) when \\(b=n\\) and forces \\(a\\in\\{1,2\\}\\) when \\(b=n-1\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the family, and exactly two lines are sunny.\n\n**One sunny line (even \\(n\\)).**  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\) and add the sunny line \\(\\ell\\) used in the odd‑\\(n\\) construction, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{\\text{even}}_1=\n\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\ell\\},\n\\qquad\n\\ell:\\;y-n=-2(x-1).\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((1,n)\\) (the only point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(b=n\\)) lies on \\(\\ell\\); all other points have \\(b\\le n-1\\) and are covered by the appropriate horizontal line. Thus the family covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nInequality (6) shows that no configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines can have more sunny lines than stated.  \nThe constructions of Sections 4.1–4.3 realise every admissible value of \\(k\\).  \nHence the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,2\\}, & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
