 second coordinate equals \\(b\\). Hence \\(\\bigcup\\mathcal H_n=T_n\\) and no line is sunny.\n\n#### 4.2  One sunny line (\\(k=1\\))\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\ell}_{\\text{sunny}},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell\\) is the line through the two lattice points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,n-2)\\).  \nIts equation is \\(y-n=-2(x-1)\\); the slope \\(-2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.\n\n*Verification of coverage.*  \n\n* The only point of \\(T_n\\) with second coordinate \\(n\\) is \\((1,n)\\); it lies on \\(\\ell\\).\n* Every other point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n-1\\) and therefore belongs to the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), which is present in the family.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nInequality (4) shows that no configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain more than one sunny line. The two explicit families constructed in §4 demonstrate that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nHence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\text{ for all }n\\ge3\\;}.\n\\]"
