            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The set of required points  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for \\(n\\) distinct lines whose union contains every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nCounting the points:\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{b=1}^{n}(n+1-b)=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  How many points can a single line contain?\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) in the integer form  \n\n\\[\nqx-py=c,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(p,q\\) are coprime integers, \\(p\\neq0\\), \\(q\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\ell\\cap T_n\\,|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) are two distinct lattice points on \\(\\ell\\), the vector \\((a'-a,b'-b)\\) is an integer multiple of the primitive direction vector \\((p,q)\\). Hence moving from one lattice point of \\(\\ell\\) to the next changes the sum \\(a+b\\) by exactly \\(|p|+|q|\\).\n\nChoose a point \\((a_0,b_0)\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\) with minimal value of \\(a+b\\).  \nIf we step one primitive vector backwards, i.e. to \\((a_0-p,b_0-q)\\), at least one of the coordinates becomes \\(\\le0\\) (otherwise the sum would be smaller than the minimal one). Consequently every other lattice point of \\(\\ell\\) that lies in \\(T_n\\) is obtained by adding non‑negative multiples of \\((p,q)\\) to \\((a_0,b_0)\\).\n\nStarting from \\((a_0,b_0)\\) and adding \\((p,q)\\) repeatedly increases the sum \\(a+b\\) by \\(|p|+|q|\\) each time. The maximal admissible sum is \\(n+1\\); therefore the number of lattice points of \\(\\ell\\) that stay inside \\(T_n\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-(a_0+b_0)}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|p|+|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|p|,|q|\\ge1\\) and \\((p,q)\\neq(1,-1)\\), we have \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\), which yields the second inequality in (2). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (non‑sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be horizontal (\\(y=b\\)), vertical (\\(x=a\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\ell\\cap T_n\\,|\\le n .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Horizontal.*  For a fixed \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); thus the line \\(y=b\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Vertical.*  By symmetry the same bound holds.\n\n*Slope \\(-1\\).*  Such a line is \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nThe admissible integer solutions of \\(a+b=c\\) are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); there are \\(c-1\\le n\\) of them. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A necessary counting inequality\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\); then \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\); by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points.  \nCounting incidences (a point that lies on several lines is counted once for each line) therefore gives\n\n\\[\nk\\bigl(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\bigr)+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{★}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (★):\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1-n\\Bigr)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n^{2}\n   =-\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1-n\\le0\\), multiplying the inequality by \\(-1\\) (which reverses the sign) yields\n\n\\[\nk\\;\\le\\;\\frac{\\,\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\,}{\\,n-\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor-1\\,}. \\tag{†}\n\\]\n\nNow evaluate the denominator.\n\n*If \\(n=2t+1\\) (odd):* \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=t\\), so the denominator equals\n\\( (2t+1)-t-1 = t\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{(2t+1)(2t)}{2t}=2t+1\\over t = 2+\\frac{1}{t}\\,,\n\\]\n\nwhich forces \\(k\\le1\\) because \\(k\\) is an integer.\n\n*If \\(n=2t\\) (even, \\(t\\ge2\\)):* \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=t\\), denominator\n\\(=2t-t-1 = t-1\\). Thus\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{2t(2t-1)}{2(t-1)} = \\frac{2t(2t-1)}{2t-2}\n      = 2+\\frac{2}{t-1}\\,,\n\\]\n\nagain giving \\(k\\le1\\) for every integer \\(t\\ge2\\).\n\nConsequently, for **all** \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le1}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Sharpness – constructions attaining the bound\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Zero sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_n=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(b\\) (\\(1\\le b\\le n\\)) the line \\(y=b\\) contains exactly the points\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); these are precisely the points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\). Hence \\(\\bigcup\\mathcal H_n=T_n\\) and no line is sunny.\n\n#### 4.2  One sunny line (\\(k=1\\))\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\ell}_{\\text{sunny}},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell\\) is the line through the two lattice points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,n-2)\\).  \nIts equation is \\(y-n=-2(x-1)\\); the slope \\(-2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.\n\n*Verification of coverage.*  \n\n* The only point of \\(T_n\\) with second coordinate \\(n\\) is \\((1,n)\\); it lies on \\(\\ell\\).\n* Every other point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n-1\\) and therefore belongs to the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), which is present in the family.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nInequality (4) shows that no configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain more than one sunny line. The two explicit families constructed in §4 demonstrate that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nHence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\text{ for all }n\\ge3\\;}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
