name the axes).  \n\nConsider the point \\(P=(v,h)\\). Since \\(P\\in T_n\\) (otherwise the covering condition would be violated), we have  \n\n\\[\nv+h\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nReplace the horizontal line \\(y=h\\) by the sunny line that passes through \\(P\\) and has slope \\(\\alpha\\) where \\(\\alpha\\) is any real number different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nDenote this new line by \\(\\ell_{\\alpha}\\).  \n\nAll points that were previously covered by \\(y=h\\) but are **not** on \\(\\ell_{\\alpha}\\) have the form \\((x,h)\\) with \\(x\\neq v\\).  \nEach such point satisfies \\(x+h\\le n+1\\) (by (6) and the fact that \\(x\\ge 1\\)).  \nConsequently the diagonal line \\(d: x+y=n+1\\) passes through every one of them.  \n\nIf the diagonal line \\(d\\) is already present in \\(\\mathcal L\\) we are done.  \nIf not, we add \\(d\\) to the family and, to keep the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\), we delete the vertical line \\(x=v\\) (which is non‑sunny).  \n\nThe resulting family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) contains exactly one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal L\\) (the line \\(\\ell_{\\alpha}\\)), still covers all points of \\(T_n\\) (by the argument above), and has the same cardinality. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Proposition 3 (Existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart with the non‑sunny covering \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) given by Lemma 1.  \n\\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) contains \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=0\\).  \n\nIf \\(k=0\\) we are finished.  \nAssume \\(0\\le k<n-2\\).  \nSince \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) has at least three non‑sunny lines, Lemma 2 can be applied to obtain a new covering \\(\\mathcal F_{1}\\) with exactly one sunny line.  \nIf \\(k=1\\) we stop; otherwise \\(\\mathcal F_{1}\\) still pos