            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (At least two non‑sunny lines are necessary)\n\nIn any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) there are at least two distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe point \\((1,1)\\in T_n\\).  \nIf a line through \\((1,1)\\) is sunny, its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and the line is not vertical.  \nFor any other point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\((a,b)\\neq(1,1)\\) we would need  \n\n\\[\nb-1=m(a-1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a,b\\) are integers, (2) forces \\(m\\) to be rational.  \nIf \\(m\\) were not \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\), the right‑hand side would be an integer only when \\(a-1\\) is a multiple of the denominator of \\(m\\); however the set \\(T_n\\) contains points with every possible value of \\(a-1\\) from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-1\\). Consequently a sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) can meet at most one other point of \\(T_n\\).  \nThus to cover all points we may assume that the line containing \\((1,1)\\) is **non‑sunny**; we may take it to be either \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) or the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nNow consider the two points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  \nBoth lie on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\), which is non‑sunny.  \nIf we used that diagonal to cover them, the point \\((1,1)\\) would still need a line, and the only non‑sunny lines through \\((1,1)\\) are \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\); at least one of these must be present in the family.  \nHence any covering contains **two distinct** non‑sunny lines (the diagonal and one of the two axes through \\((1,1)\\)). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (A covering with no sunny lines)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{0}= \\bigl\\{\\,y=h\\mid h=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\bigr\\}\n                 \\;\\cup\\;\n                 \\bigl\\{\\,x=1\\,\\bigr\\}\n                 \\;\\cup\\;\n                 \\bigl\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) are horizontal, vertical or diagonal, hence non‑sunny, and \\(|\\mathcal F_{0}|=n\\).\n\n*Claim.* \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le n-2\\)*, the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) and contains the point.\n\n*If \\(b=n-1\\) or \\(b=n\\)*, then \\(a\\le 2\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n – If \\(a=1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\n – If \\(a\\ge 2\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\); hence the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nAll possibilities are covered, so every element of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Replacing a horizontal line by a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and that contains the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(h\\) be an integer with \\(2\\le h\\le n-2\\) such that the horizontal line \\(y=h\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nChoose any sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y=\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\qquad \\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\\;\\beta\\notin\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n(For instance \\(\\alpha=2,\\;\\beta=\\sqrt2\\).)  \nBecause \\(\\beta\\) is non‑integral, \\(\\ell\\) contains **no** point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nForm  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L' = \\bigl(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\,y=h\\,\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Claim.* \\(\\mathcal L'\\) still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll points that were covered by lines other than \\(y=h\\) remain covered.  \nConsider a point \\((a,h)\\) that was covered only by the removed horizontal line.\n\n*If \\(a=1\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is still present.\n\n*If \\(a\\ge 2\\)*, then \\(a+h\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(a+h=n+1\\) the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\), which is still present.  \nIf \\(a+h<n+1\\) the point is not on the diagonal, but such a point cannot exist because for \\(h\\ge2\\) the inequality \\(a+h<n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le n-h-1\\le n-3\\); however the horizontal line \\(y=h\\) was the **only** line of slope \\(0\\) passing through \\((a,h)\\).  \nSince the diagonal does not contain \\((a,h)\\) in this case, the point must already be covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (which happens exactly when \\(a=1\\)).  \nThus every point \\((a,h)\\) remains covered after the replacement.\n\nConsequently \\(\\mathcal L'\\) is a covering of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proposition 1 (Existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart from the non‑sunny covering \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) of Lemma 2.  \n\\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) already contains the two required non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\