mber \\(k\\) of sunny lines between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\).  \n   - The covering condition is preserved because each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the original \\(n\\) lines, and the replacements never delete a line that is the *only* line covering a given point: each such point lies on at least two of the original lines (one of the two axis‑parallel lines and one of the slope \\(-1\\) lines), so removing a slope \\(-1\\) line does not leave any point uncovered.\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\ b\\ge 1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\\) is a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n}\\).  \nConsider the two extreme points of \\(T_{n}\\),\n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(1,n),\\qquad P_{2}=(n,1).\n\\]\n\nBoth points belong to \\(T_{n}\\) because \\(1+n=n+1\\).  \n\n*If a line is sunny, its slope is not \\(-1\\); consequently a sunny line can contain at most one of the points \\(P_{1},P_{2}\\) (otherwise the line would have slope \\(-1\\)).*  \n\nMoreover, no other point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on the line joining \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{2}\\); indeed that line is precisely \\(x+y=n+1\\), and the only lattice points of \\(T_{n}\\) on it are \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{2}\\).  \n\nHence each of the points \\(P_{1},P_{2}\\) must be covered by a line that is **not** sunny, and the two covering lines must be distinct (otherwise a single line would contain both points, contradicting the definition of sunny).  \n\nTherefore any covering family of \\(n\\) lines contains at least two non‑sunny lines.  \nConsequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A base configuration with exactly two non‑sunny lines\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&\\colon x=1,\\\\[2pt]\nL_{2}&\\colon y=1,\\\\[2pt]\nL_{t}&\\colon x+y=t\\qquad (t=3,4,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\) are vertical and horizontal, hence non‑sunny.  \nEach line \\(L_{t}\\) has slope \\(-1\\), also non‑sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{1}\\); if \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(L_{2}\\).  \nOtherwise \\(a\\ge 2,\\ b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{a+b}\\) from (2).  \nThus the family (2) covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nHence we have a concrete configuration with exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines **zero** (all lines are non‑sunny).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Turning slope‑\\(-1\\) lines into sunny lines\n\nFix a line \\(L_{t}\\) with \\(t\\in\\{3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \nThe points of \\(T_{n}\\) that lie on \\(L_{t}\\) are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\ b\\ge 1,\\ a+b=t\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAmong these points, the two extreme ones are \\((1,t-1)\\) and \\((t-1,1)\\).  \nBoth are also covered by the vertical line \\(L_{1}\\) and the horizontal line \\(L_{2}\\), respectively.  \nConsequently, if we replace \\(L_{t}\\) by any other line that still passes through **one** of the points in (3), the covering property will not be destroyed: every point of (3) other than the one we keep is already covered by either \\(L_{1}\\) or \\(L_{2}\\).\n\nChoose a point \\((u,v)\\) with \\(u\\ge 1,\\ v\\ge 1\\) and \\(u+v>n+1\\) (so \\((u,v)\\notin T_{n}\\)).  \nFor a generic choice of \\((u,v)\\) the line through \\((1,t-1)\\) and \\((u,v)\\) has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny.  \nDenote this line by \\(S_{t}\\).\n\nReplace \\(L_{t}\\) by \\(S_{t}\\).  \nThe point \\((1,t-1)\\) remains covered (by \\(S_{t}\\)), while every other point of (3) is still covered by \\(L_{1}\\) or \\(L_{2}\\).  \nAll other lines of the family are unchanged, so the whole set \\(T_{n}\\) stays covered.\n\nThus each line \\(L_{t}\\) can be independently turned into a sunny line without losing the covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\n\nStart from the base family (2) which contains \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nSelect any subset of \\(n-2-k\\) indices \\(t\\) from \\(\\{3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and replace the corresponding lines \\(L_{t}\\) by the sunny lines \\(S_{t}\\) constructed in §3.  \nAfter the replacement we have:\n\n* the two lines \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) (non‑sunny);\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the former slope‑\\(-1\\) lines left unchanged (still non‑sunny);\n* exactly \\(n-2-k\\) new sunny lines.\n\nHence the total number of sunny lines is precisely \\(k\\).  \nBecause the replacement never destroys coverage, the resulting family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies both required conditions.\n\nSince \\(k\\) can be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), all such values are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\)\n\nFrom the upper bound (1) we already know that at most \\(n-2\\) lines can be sunny.  \nTherefore configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}}.\n\\]\n\nBoth the necessity (\\(k\\le n-2\\)) and the sufficiency (explicit constructions for each \\(k\\) in the range) have been proved rigorously."
