\) and \\(P_{2}\\).\n\nIf a line is sunny its slope is not \\(-1\\); therefore a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the points \\(P_{1},P_{2}\\).  \nConsequently each of \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{2}\\) must be covered by a line that is **not** sunny, and the two covering lines must be distinct (otherwise a single line would contain both points, forcing slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\nThus any covering family contains at least two non\u2011sunny lines, whence\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A base configuration with two non\u2011sunny lines\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&\\colon x=1,\\\\\nL_{2}&\\colon y=1,\\\\\nL_{t}&\\colon x+y=t+1\\qquad (t=2,3,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\) are vertical and horizontal, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nEach line \\(L_{t}\\) has slope \\(-1\\), also non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{1}\\).  \n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{2}\\).  \n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); set \\(t=a+b-1\\) (so \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\)).  \n  Then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=t+1\\), i.e. it lies on \\(L_{t}\\).\n\nHence the family (2) covers every point of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nAll \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny, so this configuration realises \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Replacing a slope\u2011\\(-1\\) line by a sunny line\n\nFix a line \\(L_{t}\\) from (2) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\).  \nIts required points are  \n\n\\[\nR_{t}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b=t+1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAmong them the two extreme points are \\((1,t)\\) and \\((t,1)\\).  \nThese are already covered by \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\), respectively.\n\nChoose any point \\((u,v)\\) with \\(u\\ge1,\\;v\\ge1\\) and \\(u+v>n+1\\) (so \\((u,v)\\n