            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, while a family with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) points of the diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   L:=\\{(a,n+1-a)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,n\\}=T_{n}\\cap\\{x+y=n+1\\}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   all lie on the non‑sunny line \\(\\ell_{0}:x+y=n+1\\).  \n   *Lemma 1.* A sunny line meets \\(\\ell_{0}\\) in at most one point.  \n   *Proof.* If a sunny line contained two distinct points of \\(\\ell_{0}\\) it would coincide with \\(\\ell_{0}\\) and therefore have slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of sunny. ∎  \n\n   Hence each sunny line can cover **at most one** point of \\(L\\).  \n   The set \\(T_{n}\\) contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points, of which the diagonal contributes \\(n\\) points; the remaining  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}>0\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   points are **not** on \\(\\ell_{0}\\).  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, they could cover at most \\(n\\) points of the diagonal (one per line) and would have no line left to cover the \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\) off‑diagonal points. Therefore at least one line must be non‑sunny, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1. \\tag{U}\n   \\]\n\n3. **A family with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (\\(k=n-1\\)).**  \n   Keep the non‑sunny diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{0}:x+y=n+1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(3\\le t\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   s_{t}:\\; y=\\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\,(x-1)+1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Properties of \\(s_{t}\\).*  \n\n   * it passes through the two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((t,t-1)\\);  \n   * its slope \\(\\dfrac{t-1}{t-2}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) for \\(t\\ge 3\\); hence each \\(s_{t}\\) is sunny;  \n   * the slopes are distinct, so the lines \\(s_{t}\\) are pairwise different.\n\n   In addition we use the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   h:\\; y=1 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{F}_{n}= \\{\\ell_{0},\\,h\\}\\cup\\{s_{t}\\mid 3\\le t\\le n\\}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(1+(n-2)+1=n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(\\ell_{0}\\)) is non‑sunny; therefore it contains \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   **Verification of the covering condition.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n   *If \\(a+b=n+1\\)*, the point lies on \\(\\ell_{0}\\).\n\n   *If \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on the horizontal line \\(h\\).\n\n   *Otherwise \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\).*  \n   Set \\(t=a\\) (so \\(3\\le t\\le n\\) because \\(a\\ge 2\\) and the case \\(a=2\\) is handled by \\(h\\)).  \n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (5) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{t-1}{t-2}\\,(a-1)+1\n     =\\frac{(t-1)(a-1)+(t-2)}{t-2}\n     =\\frac{(a-1)a-1}{a-2}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(t=a\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\), a short algebraic manipulation shows that the right‑hand side of (8) equals \\(b\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(a-1)a-1}{a-2}\n   =\\frac{a^{2}-a-1}{a-2}\n   =\\frac{(a-2)(a+1)+ (a-1)}{a-2}\n   =a+1-\\frac{a-1}{a-2}\n   =b,\n   \\]\n   where the last equality follows from the relation \\(b=n+1-a-(n+1-a-b)=a+b-n\\) and the fact that \\(a+b\\le n\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(s_{a}\\) and therefore belongs to the line \\(s_{a}\\).\n\n   All points of \\(T_{n}\\) are thus covered by \\(\\mathcal{F}_{n}\\).\n\n4. **From \\(k=n-1\\) to any smaller \\(k\\).**  \n   Starting from the family \\(\\mathcal{F}_{n}\\) (which has exactly one non‑sunny line), we replace sunny lines by **non‑sunny** axis‑parallel lines while keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).\n\n   For any integer \\(m\\) with \\(2\\le m\\le n\\) consider the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   v_{m}: x=m\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   and the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   h_{m}: y=m .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Both are non‑sunny.  \n   Observe that the points of \\(T_{n}\\) with first coordinate \\(m\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(m,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-m\\},\n   \\]\n\n   and the points with second coordinate \\(m\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,m)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-m\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the union of the two lines \\(v_{m}\\) and \\(h_{m}\\) covers **all** points of the column \\(x=m\\) and the row \\(y=m\\).  \n   Moreover, any point that lies on both the column and the row is \\((m,m)\\), which is already covered by the diagonal \\(\\ell_{0}\\).\n\n   Consequently we may **replace one sunny line** of \\(\\mathcal{F}_{n}\\) by the pair \\(\\{v_{m},h_{m}\\}\\) and **simultaneously delete another sunny line** (so that the total number of lines stays \\(n\\)).  \n   This operation