ible \\(y\\).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the lines\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{F}_{n}= \\{\\ell_{0}\\}\\cup\\{s_{a}\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{v_{n-1}\\},\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   which consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-2\\) of them sunny.  \n   Adding the sunny line \\(s_{n-2}\\) (whose slope equals \\(1\\)) we obtain a family with **\\(n-1\\) sunny lines**:\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{F}^{\\,*}_{n}= \\{\\ell_{0}\\}\\cup\\{s_{a}\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-1\\}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Here the line \\(s_{n-1}\\) coincides with \\(v_{n-1}\\) and is non‑sunny; all the other \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.  \n   Thus the case \\(k=n-1\\) is realised.\n\n4. **From \\(k=n-1\\) to any smaller \\(k\\).**  \n   Starting from the family \\(\\mathcal{F}^{\\,*}_{n}\\) (which has exactly one non‑sunny line, namely \\(\\ell_{0}\\)), we replace sunny lines by additional non‑sunny axis‑parallel lines while preserving the covering property.\n\n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-1\\) introduce the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   v_{c}: x=c ,\\qquad h_{c}: y=c . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Both lines belong to the set of admissible lines and together they cover **all** points of the column \\(x=c\\) and the row \\(y=c\\) that lie in \\(T_{n}\\); the unique intersection \\((c,c)\\) is already covered by the diagonal \\(\\ell_{0}\\).\n\n   **Replacement operation.**  \n   Suppose the current family contains a sunny line \\(L\\).  \n   Choose a number \\(c\\) such that neither \\(v_{c}\\) nor \\(h_{c}\\) is already present.  \n   Delete the sunny line \\(L\\) and insert the two non‑sunny lines \\(v_{c}\\) and \\(h_{c}\\).  \n   The total number of lines increases by one; to keep the total at \\(n\\) we simultaneously delete **any** other non‑sunny line (for instance the diagonal \\(\\ell_{0}\\) if it has not been deleted yet).  \n   The net effect is a reduction of the sunny count by exactly one, while every point of \\(T_{n}\\) remains covered:\n\n   * Points that were on the removed sunny line are either on the diagonal (still covered) or lie in the column or row of the chosen \\(c\\) (now covered by \\(v_{c}\\) or \\(h_{c}\\)).  \n   * All other points are untouched.\n\n   Repeating this operation \\(n-1-k\\) times transforms \\(\\mathcal{F}^{\\,*}_{n}\\) into a family with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   The extreme case \\(k=0\\) is obtained after \\(n-1\\) repetitions; one concrete family is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{\\ell_{0}\\}\\cup\\{v_{1},v_{2},\\dots ,v_{n-1}\\},\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the diagonal together with the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n   All these lines are non‑sunny and they clearly cover every point of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   The argument of step 2 shows that at least one line must be non‑sunny; therefore a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny cannot exist. This establishes the upper bound (U).\n\n6. **Conclusion**  \n   Combining the upper bound \\(k\\le n-1\\) with the explicit constructions for every integer \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), we obtain the complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   The proof is fully rigorous: the upper bound uses a counting argument based on Lemma 1, and the constructions verify the covering condition for every required value of \\(k\\)."
