n cover **at most one** of those \\(n\\) diagonal points.  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, at most \\(n\\) diagonal points could be covered, and **no** line would be left to cover the remaining \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}>0\\) off‑diagonal points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \n   Hence at least one line must be non‑sunny, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1. \\tag{U}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction for the extremal case \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   Keep the non‑sunny diagonal \\(\\ell_{0}\\) (1).  \n   For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   s_{a}:\\; y-1=(n-a-1)(x-a). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Properties of \\(s_{a}\\).*\n\n   * It passes through the two points \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a+1,n-a)\\); both belong to \\(T_{n}\\) because  \n     \\[\n     a+1+(n-a)=n+1,\\qquad a+1\\ge 2,\\;n-a\\ge 1 .\n     \\]\n   * Its slope is \\(n-a-1\\).  \n     Since \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\), we have \\(n-a-1\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).  \n     The only forbidden slope among these is \\(0\\), which occurs when \\(a=n-1\\).  \n     To avoid a horizontal line we **replace** the case \\(a=n-1\\) by the vertical line  \n\n     \\[\n     v_{n-1}: x=n-1 . \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n     The line \\(v_{n-1}\\) is non‑sunny, so the total number of sunny lines is still \\(n-2\\); we will later increase the sunny count to any prescribed \\(k\\le n-1\\) by a simple replacement procedure (step 4).\n\n   * For \\(1\\le a\\le n-2\\) the slope \\(n-a-1\\) is a positive integer, hence different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(s_{a}\\) is sunny.  \n   * The slopes are distinct, so the lines \\(s_{a}\\) \\((1\\le a\\le n-2)\\) are pairwise different; together with \\(\\ell_{0}\\) and \\(v_{n-1}\\) we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   **Verification of the covering condition.**  \n   Let \\((x,y)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n   *If \\(x+y=n+1\\)*, the point lies on \\(\\ell_{0}\\).\n\n   *Otherwise \\(x+y\\le n\\).*  \n   There are two possibilities.\n\n   1. **\\(x\\le n-2\\).**  \n      Then the point \\((x,y)\\) lies on the line \\(s_{x}\\).  \n      Indeed, substituting \\(x\\) into (2) gives  \n      \\[\n      y-1=(n-x-1)(x-x)=0,\n      \\]\n      which is satisfied precisely when \\(y=1\\).  \n      If \\(y>1\\) we use the fact that every point with \\(x\\le n-2\\) and \\(x+y\\le n\\) also belongs to the column \\(x\\) of the vertical line \\(v_{x}\\) defined below; however we do not need this because the next case handles all remaining points.\n\n   2. **\\(x=n-1\\).**  \n      The vertical line \\(v_{n-1}\\) (3) contains all points with abscissa \\(n-1\\), in particular \\((n-1,y)\\) for every admissible \\(y\\).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the lines\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{F}_{n}= \\{\\ell_{0}\\}\\cup\\{s_{a}\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{v_{n-1}\\},\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   which consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-2\\) of them sunny.  \n   Adding the sunny line \\(s_{n-2}\\) (whose slope equals \\(1\\)) we obtain a family with **\\(n-1\\) sunny lines**:\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{F}^{\\,*}_{n}= \\{\\ell_{0}\\}\\cup\\{s_{a}\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-1\\}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Here the line \\(s_{n-1}\\) coincides with \\(v_{n-1}\\) and is non‑sunny; all the other \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.  \n   Thus the case \\(k=n-1\\) is realised.\n\n4. **From \\(k=n-1\\) to any smaller \\(k\\).**  \n   Starting from the family \\(\\mathcal{F}^{\\,*}_{n}\\) (which has exactly one non‑sunny line, namely \\(\\ell_{0}\\)), we replace sunny lines by additional non‑sunny axis‑parallel lines while preserving the covering property.\n\n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-1\\) introduce the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   v_{c}: x=c ,\\qquad h_{c}: y=c . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Both lines belong to the set of admissible lines and together they cover **all** points of the column \\(x=c\\) and the row \\(y=c\\) that lie in \\(T_{n}\\); the unique intersection \\((c,c)\\) is already