 and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n4. **All values of \\(k\\).**  \n   Since the construction works for every integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n\\), every such \\(k\\) can be realised. No further restriction can appear, because the trivial construction for \\(k=0\\) already shows that the lower bound \\(k\\ge 0\\) is sharp, and the above replacement shows that the upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) is also sharp.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_{n}\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_{n}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A family with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{d}:\\;x+y=d .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(L_{d}\\) are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore none of them is sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) then \\(a+b=d\\) for a unique \\(d\\) in the above range, so \\((a,b)\\in L_{d}\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_{d}\\}_{d=2}^{n+1}\\) satisfy the covering condition, and we have exhibited a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Replacing diagonals by sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,t\\}\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y=\\frac{1}{i}\\,x+(i+1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{1}{i}\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny.\n\n**Lemma 2.1.**  \nFor a given \\(i\\) the set of points of \\(S_{i}\\) that belong to \\(S_{n}\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i+j,\\;i+1+j)\\mid j=0,1,\\dots ,n-i-1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWriting (2) as \\(y-i-1=\\frac{1}{i}(x-i)\\) we obtain \\(x-i = i(y-i-1)\\).  \nHence integer solutions \\((x,y)\\) satisfy \\(x-i\\) divisible by \\(i\\); put \\(x-i=ij\\) with \\(j\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  \nThen \\(y-i-1=j\\), i.e. \\(y=i+1+j\\) and \\(x=i+ij=i+j\\).  \nThe conditions \\(x\\ge 1,\\;y\\ge 1\\) are automatic for \\(j\\ge 0\\).  \nFinally \\(x+y = (i+j)+(i+1+j)=2i+1+2j\\le n+1\\) gives \\(j\\le n-i-1\\). \u220e\n\nThus each sunny line \\(S_{i}\\) contains exactly the points whose coordinates differ by \\(1\\) and whose sum runs from \\(2i+1\\) up to \\(n+1\\).\n\nObserve that the diagonal line \\(L_{2i+1}\\) (the line \\(x+y=2i+1\\)) contains precisely the points \\((i+j,i+1+j)\\) with the same range of \\(j\\). Consequently the set of points of \\(S_{i}\\) that lie in \\(S_{n}\\) coincides with the set of points of the diagonal \\(L_{2i+1}\\) that lie in \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nTake the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}_{t}= \\{S_{1},S_{2},\\dots ,S_{t}\\}\\cup\\{L_{d}\\mid d\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{2,4,\\dots ,2t\\}\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn words: we keep all diagonals \\(L_{d}\\) except those with odd index \\(d=2i+1\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,t\\); each removed diagonal is replaced by the sunny line \\(S_{i}\\).  \n\n- The family contains exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines (the \\(S_{i}\\)\u2019s) and \\(n-t\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the remaining diagonals).  \n- By Lemma\u202f2.1, for each \\(i\\) the points covered by \\(S_{i}\\) are precisely those that were covered by the removed diagonal \\(L_{2i+1}\\).  \n- All other points of \\(S_{n}\\) are covered by the untouched diagonals.\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal{F}_{t}\\) covers every point of \\(S_{n}\\) and uses exactly \\(k=t\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince \\(t\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), we have exhibited, for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Exhaustiveness\n\nThe definition of a sunny line excludes only three directions.  \nAny line that is not parallel to one of those three directions is admissible, so there is no inherent obstruction to having any number of sunny lines up to the total of \\(n\\).  \nThe construction above shows that every such number is indeed attainable; therefore no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
