= \\frac{1}{\\,n-i\\,}>0,\n   \\]\n\n   which is neither \\(0\\) (horizontal), nor undefined (vertical), nor \\(-1\\).  Hence this line is sunny.  Moreover it contains **all** points of \\(H_i\\): any \\((a,i)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-i\\) lies on the line through \\(Q_1\\) and \\(Q_3\\) because the three points are collinear and the \\(x\\)-coordinates run consecutively from \\(1\\) to \\(n+1-i\\).  Consequently we may replace the horizontal line \\(y=i\\) by the sunny line \\(S_i\\) without losing any covered point.\n\n3. **Turning a vertical line into a sunny line.**  \n   For a fixed \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) on the vertical line \\(x=c\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   V_c=\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Choose the two extreme points of \\(V_c\\),\n\n   \\[\n   R_1=(c,1),\\qquad R_2=(c,n+1-c),\n   \\]\n\n   and replace the vertical line \\(x=c\\) by the sunny line \\(T_c\\) that passes through  \n\n   \\[\n   R_1,\\;R_2,\\;(c+1,1).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of the line through \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c+1,1)\\) is \\(0\\) (horizontal), which is forbidden; instead we use the line through \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c+1,n+2-c)\\).  The point \\((c+1,n+2-c)\\) belongs to \\(T_n\\) because  \n\n   \\[\n   (c+1)+(n+2-c)=n+3\\le n+1\\quad\\text{fails},\n   \\]\n\n   so we must adjust.  The correct construction is:\n\n   *Take the two extreme points of \\(V_c\\) and one point one unit to the right of the upper endpoint:*  \n\n   \\[\n   S_1=(c,1),\\qquad S_2=(c,n+1-c),\\qquad S_3=(c+1,n+1-c).\n   \\]\n\n   The line through \\(S_1\\) and \\(S_3\\) has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m_c=\\frac{(n+1-c)-1}{(c+1)-c}=n-c,\n   \\]\n\n   which is a positive integer \\(\\ge 1\\); hence it is sunny.  Because the three points are collinear, every intermediate point \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\) lies on this line, so the whole set \\(V_c\\) remains covered after the replacement.\n\n4. **Realising any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n   Begin with the base covering (step\u202f1).  \n   For each index \\(i\\) among a chosen set \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) replace the horizontal line \\(y=i\\) by the sunny line \\(S_i\\) of step\u202f2.  \n   For each index \\(c\\) among a chosen set \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) replace the vertical line \\(x=c\\) by the sunny line \\(T_c\\) of step\u202f3.  \n\n   The families of replacements are independent: a point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to exactly one horizontal line and exactly one vertical line, and each replacement keeps **all** points that were on the removed line.  Consequently after performing all replacements the set of lines still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n   The total number of sunny lines obtained is \\(|I|+|C|\\).  By choosing \\(I\\) and \\(C\\) so that \\(|I|+|C|=k\\) we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Because \\(0\\le |I|+|C|\\le (n-1)+n = 2n-1\\) we can certainly realise every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) (take all sunny lines of one type, at most \\(n\\) of them).\n\n5. **Upper bound.**  \n   Any configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  Hence \\(k\\le n\\) is necessary.\n\nCombining the construction (which shows that every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable) with the trivial upper bound yields the desired classification.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe shall prove:\n\n> **Theorem.**  \n> For a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of integers \\(k\\ge0\\) for which there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying  \n> (i) \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}L\\supseteq T_n\\),  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny,  \n> is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. A covering with no sunny lines  \n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i^{(0)}:\\; y=i\\quad (i=1,\\dots ,n-1),\\qquad L_n^{(0)}:\\; x=1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non\u2011sunny (the first \\(n-1\\) are horizontal, the last one is vertical).\n\n*Claim.*  \\(\\{L_i^{(0)}\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Because \\(b\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\le n-1\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)), the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), i.e. on \\(L_b^{(0)}\\).  If \\(b=n\\) then necessarily \\(a=1\\) (since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)), and the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1=L_n^{(0)}\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the family. \u220e\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Replacing a horizontal line by a sunny line  \n\nFix \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  The points of \\(T_n\\) on the line \\(y=i\\) are  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(a,i)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; \\text{through }(1,i)\\text{ and }(n+1-i,i+1).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{(i+1)-i}{(n+1-i)-1}= \\frac{1}{\\,n-i\\,}>0 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(S_i\\) is **sunny** (its direction is neither horizontal, vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\nBecause the three points \\((1,i),\\;(n+1-i,i),\\;(n+1-i,i+1)\\) are collinear, the line \\(S_i\\) contains the whole set \\(H_i\\): any point \\((a,i)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-i\\) lies on the line through \\((1,i)\\) and \\((n+1-i,i+1)\\).  Consequently we may replace the horizontal line \\(y=i\\) by the sunny line \\(S_i\\) without losing any point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Replacing a vertical line by a sunny line  \n\nFix \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  The points of \\(T_n\\) on the line \\(x=c\\) are  \n\n\\[\nV_c=\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nT_c:\\; \\text{through }(c,1)\\text{ and }(c+1,n+1-c).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe second point belongs to \\(T_n\\) because  \n\n\\[\n(c+1)+(n+1-c)=n+2\\le n+1\\quad\\text{fails},\n\\]\n\nso we must adjust the construction.  The correct line is\n\n\\[\nU_c:\\; \\text{through }(c,1)\\text{ and }(c+1,n-c).\n\\tag{5'}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(c\\le n\\), we have \\(n-c\\ge0\\) and  \n\n\\[\nc+1+(n-c)=n+1,\n\\]\n\nso \\((c+1,n-c)\\in T_n\\).  The slope of \\(U_c\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_c=\\frac{(n-c)-1}{(c+1)-c}=n-c-1\\ge0 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(c=n\\) then \\(U_n\\) is the horizontal line \\(y=0\\), which does not belong to \\(T_n\\); in this extreme case we simply keep the original vertical line \\(x=n\\) (it is non\u2011sunny) and never need to replace it.  For every \\(c\\le n-1\\) the slope \\(m_c\\) is a non\u2011negative integer different from \\(0\\) (unless \\(c=n-1\\), when \\(m_c=0\\) \u2013 this is the horizontal line \\(y=0\\) which is forbidden).  To avoid the forbidden slope we instead use the line through \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c+1,n-c-1)\\); its slope is \\(n-c-2\\ge1\\) for \\(c\\le n-3\\).  For the remaining small values of \\(c\\) we treat them individually:\n\n* \\(c=n-2\\): use the line through \\((n-2,1)\\) and \\((n-1,1)\\) \u2013 this is horizontal, so we keep the original vertical line.\n* \\(c=n-1\\): keep the original vertical line \\(x=n-1\\).\n\nThus for every \\(c\\) we can either keep the vertical line (non\u2011sunny) or replace it by a sunny line that still contains **all** points of \\(V_c\\).  The explicit replacement needed only for \\(c\\le n-3\\) is the line through \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c+1,n-c-2)\\); its slope is \\(n-c-2\\ge1\\), hence sunny, and the three points \\((c,1),\\;(c,n+1-c),\\;(c+1,n-c-2)\\) are collinear, guaranteeing that every intermediate point \\((c,b)\\) lies on the line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nStart from the base