ng (1) we have \\(|S_{n}|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  \n   For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}> n^{2}-n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed,\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n^{2}-n+1)=\\frac{-n^{2}+3n-2}{2}\n   =\\frac{-(n-1)(n-2)}{2}>0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (3) is therefore impossible.  The assumption that a sunny line belongs to a covering family of size \\(n\\) leads to a contradiction.\n\n   Consequently **any family of \\(n\\) lines that covers all points of \\(S_{n}\\) must contain no sunny line**.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction in step\u202f2 shows that \\(k=0\\) can be realized.  \n   Step\u202f4 proves that no covering family of \\(n\\) lines can contain a sunny line, i.e. no covering family can have \\(k\\ge 1\\).  \n\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]"
