dinates, so the segment joining them has slope \\(-1\\). A line whose slope differs from \\(-1\\) cannot contain two such points; therefore it meets each diagonal in at most one point. There are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals, giving the bound. ∎\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the diagonals, then either \\(\\ell=D_{d}\\) for some integer \\(d\\in[2,n+1]\\) (in which case \\(|\\ell\\cap S_{n}|=d-1\\)), or \\(\\ell\\) contains no point of \\(S_{n}\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nChoose a subset  \n\n\\[\nI\\subset\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\},\\qquad |I|=k .\n\\]\n\nThe diagonals with indices in \\(I\\) will be replaced by sunny lines; the remaining diagonals will stay unchanged.\n\nFor each \\(d\\in I\\) we shall construct a sunny line \\(\\ell_{d}\\) that\n\n* passes through the point \\((1,d-1)\\) (which lies on the diagonal \\(D_{d}\\));\n* meets **every** retained diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) (\\(c\\notin I\\)) in exactly one point, and these intersection points are all distinct for different values of \\(d\\).\n\nIf such lines exist, the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\bigl\\{\\ell_{d}\\mid d\\in I\\bigr\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{D_{c}\\mid c\\notin I\\bigr\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ncontains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny diagonals, and every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies either on a retained diagonal or on one of the sunny lines, i.e. \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies the covering requirement.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Hall‑type selection of intersection points\n\nFix \\(d\\in I\\).  \nConsider the bipartite graph \\(G_{d}\\) defined as follows.\n\n* Left part \\(L\\): the set of retained diagonals \\(\\{D_{c}\\mid c\\notin I\\}\\) (there are \\(n-k\\) of them).\n* Right part \\(R\\): the set of points of \\(S_{n}\\) that lie on those retained diagonals.\n* An edge connects a diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) to a point \\(P\\in D_{c}\\) iff the line through \\(P\\) and the base point \\((1,d-1)\\) is **not** parallel to the diagonals.\n\nBecause the base point has sum \\(d\\) and any point \\(P\\) on a different diagonal has sum \\(c\\neq d\\), the line \\( \\overline{(1,d-1)P}\\) has slope different from \\(-1\\); consequently every such line is sunny. Hence **every** point of a retained diagonal is adjacent to the base point, i.e. the graph is complete bipartite.\n\nWe now apply Hall’s marriage theorem to obtain a system of distinct representatives: we need a set of \\(n-k\\) edges, one incident with each left vertex, such that the right‑hand endpoints are all different.  \n\nSince the graph is complete bipartite, Hall’s condition is trivially satisfied:\n\n\\[\n\\forall\\,\\mathcal C\\subseteq L,\\qquad\n\\bigl|N(\\mathcal C)\\bigr|\n= \\sum_{D_{c}\\in\\mathcal C}|D_{c}|\n\\ge |\\mathcal C| ,\n\\]\n\nbecause each retained diagonal contains at least one lattice point (\\(c\\ge 2\\)).  \nTherefore a matching of size \\(n-k\\) exists.  \n\nChoose such a matching; for each retained diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) let \\(P_{c}\\) be the matched point.  \nDefine \\(\\ell_{d}\\) to be the line through the base point \\((1,d-1)\\) and the collection \\(\\{P_{c}\\mid c\\notin I\\}\\).  \nBy construction \\(\\ell_{d}\\) meets every retained diagonal in exactly one point, and it is sunny (its slope is not \\(-1\\)).  \n\nRepeating the same procedure for every \\(d\\in I\\) yields the family of sunny lines \\(\\{\\ell_{d}\\}_{d\\in I}\\) required in (3).  \nThe matchings for different values of \\(d\\) can be chosen independently; distinctness of the sunny lines is guaranteed because each contains a different base point \\((1,d-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Verification of the covering property\n\n*Every point of a retained diagonal* \\(D_{c}\\) lies on the unique sunny line \\(\\ell_{d}\\) that was matched to \\(D_{c}\\) for the particular \\(d\\in I\\) used in the construction.  \n*Every point of a removed diagonal* \\(D_{d}\\) (\\(d\\in I\\)) lies on the base point \\((1,d-1)\\) of the corresponding sunny line \\(\\ell_{d}\\).  \n\nHence each point of \\(S_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of the family \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThe family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(\\ell_{d}\\)’s) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny diagonals, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited:\n\n* a covering with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines (the \\(n\\) diagonals);\n* for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) a covering containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, obtained by the Hall‑type construction above.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
