            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|S_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\) (i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A covering with no sunny line\n\nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_{d}:\\;x+y=d .\n\\]\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{D_{d}\\}_{d=2}^{n+1}\\) are pairwise distinct, each is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore not sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) then \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique diagonal \\(D_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(S_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines. Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A bound for a sunny line\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, then \\(|\\ell\\cap S_{n}|\\le n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll diagonals \\(D_{d}\\) have slope \\(-1\\). Two distinct points of \\(S_{n}\\) that belong to the same diagonal therefore determine a line of slope \\(-1\\). A sunny line has a different slope, so it can contain at most one point from each diagonal. There are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals, whence the bound. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of having a sunny line\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nI=\\{d\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\mid D_{d}\\notin\\mathcal L\\},\n\\qquad |I|=k .\n\\]\n\nThus the non‑sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are precisely the diagonals \\(\\{D_{d}\\mid d\\notin I\\}\\).\n\nThe number of points of \\(S_{n}\\) lying on those diagonals equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{d\\notin I}(d-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\sum_{d\\in I}(d-1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll remaining points must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines. By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points, so the total number of points that can be covered by the sunny lines is at most \\(k n\\). Hence from (4)\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\sum_{d\\in I}(d-1)\\le k n . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe smallest possible value of \\(\\sum_{d\\in I} d\\) occurs when \\(I\\) consists of the \\(k\\) smallest integers in \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{d\\in I} d\\ge 2+3+\\dots +(k+1)=\\frac{k(k+3)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{d\\in I}(d-1)=\\sum_{d\\in I} d - k\\ge \\frac{k(k+3)}{2}-k=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nInsert this lower bound into (5):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\le k n .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(2\\) and rearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nn^{2}+n-2kn\\le k^{2}+k,\n\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\n(n-k)^{2}\\le 0 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(n-k=0\\), i.e. \\(k=n\\).  \n\nIf \\(k=n\\) then every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is sunny. By Lemma 1 each such line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\); the whole family can therefore cover at most \\(n\\cdot n=n^{2}\