|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Suppose we have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Let \\(d=n-k\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \n   Each non‑sunny line can be of any slope except \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); however, the only lines that can contain **more than one** point of a given \\(P_s\\) are the diagonal lines \\(L_s\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  Consequently every non‑sunny line that is not one of the \\(L_s\\) can also cover at most one point of each \\(P_s\\).\n\n   Hence **any** line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) (sunny or not) can cover at most one point from each set \\(P_s\\), **except** the special diagonal lines \\(L_s\\), each of which covers all \\(|P_s|=s-1\\) points of its own \\(P_s\\).\n\n   Let \\(m\\) be the number of diagonal lines among the \\(d\\) non‑sunny lines.  Those \\(m\\) diagonal lines together cover  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{m}(s_i-1)\n   \\]\n\n   points, where the indices \\(s_i\\) are distinct elements of \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  The remaining \\(d-m\\) non‑sunny lines and the \\(k\\) sunny lines can each contribute at most one point from each of the \\(n\\) different sets \\(P_s\\); therefore they can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (k+d-m)\\,n = (n-m)\\,n\n   \\]\n\n   additional points.\n\n   Altogether the family can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl(\\sum_{i=1}^{m}(s_i-1)\\Bigr) + (n-m)n .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Since all \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points must be covered, inequality (1) must be an equality with the left‑hand side at least \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n\n4. **Maximising the covered points.**  \n   The sum \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{m}(s_i-1)\\) is maximised when the chosen indices are the largest possible, i.e. \\(s_i=n+2-i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,m\\).  In that case  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{m}(s_i-1)=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}(n+1-i)=m(n+1)-\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting this into (1) gives the maximal number of points that can be covered with exactly \\(m\\) diagonal lines:\n\n   \\[\n   m(n+1)-\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}+(n-m)n\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}+ \\frac{m( n-m)}{2}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The extra term \\(\\frac{m(n-m)}{2}\\) is non‑negative and is zero **only** when \\(m=0\\) or \\(m=n\\).\n\n   Because we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, we cannot have \\(m=n\\) (that would leave no room for any sunny line, i.e. \\(k=0\\), which is allowed).  If \\(m\\ge 1\\) then the right‑hand side of (2) is **strictly larger** than \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\); however the left‑hand side of (2) is an upper bound, not a lower bound, so the only way to achieve the required exact number \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) is to have the extra term vanish, i.e. \\(m=0\\).\n\n   Therefore **no diagonal line can be present** in a feasible family; all \\(n\\) lines must be non‑diagonal.  But a non‑diagonal line can meet at most one point of each \\(P_s\\), so with \\(n\\) such lines we can cover at most \\(n\\cdot n = n^{2}\\) points.  Since  \n\n   \\[\n   n^{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\qquad\\text{for }n\\ge 3,\n   \\]\n\n   this is impossible.  The only way to reach the required total is to use **exactly** the diagonal lines \\(L_2,L_3,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\); they are \\(n\\) distinct lines, they cover every point of \\(T_n\\), and none of them is sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The unique family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{\\,L_s : x+y=s,\\; s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which contains **no** sunny lines.  Hence the only admissible value of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k=0}.\n   \\]\n\n   No configuration with \\(k\\ge 1\\) exists, and the configuration with \\(k=0\\) clearly exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each c