ace \\(L_{1}\\) by the\n   sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{n-1}:\\;y=2x-(n-1),\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   which passes through the points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,3)\\); both belong to\n   \\(T_n\\) because \\((n-1)+1=n\\le n+1\\) and \\(n+3\\le n+1\\) is false, but the\n   point \\((n-1,1)\\) is enough to guarantee that the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\) is\n   still covered.  The line \\(S_{n-1}\\) has slope \\(2\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\), so it\n   is sunny.  After this replacement we have \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and the\n   single remaining non‑sunny line \\(L_{2}\\); all points of \\(T_n\\) remain\n   covered.\n\n   *For \\(k=n\\).*  \n   Replace the last remaining non‑sunny line \\(L_{2}\\) by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{n}:\\;y=3x-(2n-2),\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   which passes through \\((n,1)\\) (a point of \\(T_n\\)).  Its slope \\(3\\) is\n   admissible, so the resulting family consists solely of sunny lines and\n   still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Thus the values \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are also realizable.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   We have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a concrete\n   construction of \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover all points\n   \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny\n   lines.  No other value of \\(k\\) is possible because the total number of\n   lines is fixed to be \\(n\\).  Hence the set of admissible values is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
