\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).**  \n\n   Fix a diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  \n   By the bijection of step 4 there is a unique index \\(i\\) with \\(s=s_{i}\\).\n\n   *If \\(i\\le k\\)*, then \\(L_{i}\\in\\mathcal L_{k}\\) and, by (6), \\(L_{i}\\) contains the\n   lattice point \\((a_{i},s-a_{i})\\) of \\(D_{s}\\).\n\n   *If \\(i>k\\)*, then \\(V_{i}\\in\\mathcal L_{k}\\) and, because \\(i\\le s-1\\) (this follows from\n   \\(s\\equiv1\\pmod{i+1}\\) and \\(s\\le n+1\\)), the point \\((i,s-i)\\) belongs to both\n   \\(V_{i}\\) and \\(D_{s}\\).\n\n   Thus **each diagonal \\(D_{s}\\) contains at least one point that belongs to a line of\n   \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\)**.\n\n   The remaining points of \\(D_{s}\\) are exactly the set\n   \\(\\{(t,s-t)\\mid t=1,\\dots ,s-1,\\;t\\neq a_{i}\\text{ (if }i\\le k\\text{)}\\text{ or }t\\neq i\\text{ (if }i>k\\)}\\).\n   Every such point has its \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(t\\) satisfying \\(2\\le t\\le n\\); consequently the\n   vertical line \\(V_{t}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) (unless \\(t\\le k\\), in which case\n   the point is already covered by the sunny line \\(L_{t}\\), because for \\(t\\le k\\) we have\n   \\(s\\equiv1\\pmod{t+1}\\) exactly when \\(s=s_{t}\\), i.e. when the point is the one supplied\n   by \\(L_{t}\\)). Hence **every lattice point of \\(D_{s}\\) lies on at least one line of\n   \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\)**.\n\n   Since the diagonals \\(D_{2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) partition \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\), the family\n   \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) covers the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n8. **Impossibility of larger \\(k\\).**  \n   The configuration must contain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so it cannot contain\n   more than \\(n\\) sunny lines. Hence no integer \\(k>n\\) can be realised.\n\n9. **Conclusion.**  \n   For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) defined in\n   (8) satisfies both requirements of the problem, and no larger \\(k\\) is possible.\n   Therefore the