            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the chosen lines.\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (sunny covering of a triangle)\n\nFor every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) the \\(m\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (m+1-i),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,m \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nare distinct, sunny, and together they cover all points of \\(T_m\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i\\); hence it is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\) nor infinite, so each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nIf \\(i\\neq j\\) then the constant terms \\(m+1-i\\) and \\(m+1-j\\) differ, therefore the lines are distinct.\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\). Because \\(a+b\\le m+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb = (m+1-a) - (a-1).\n\\]\n\nSet \\(i=a\\) (note that \\(1\\le a\\le m\\)). Substituting \\(i=a\\) into (1) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = a\\,x + (m+1-a).\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) yields \\(y = a\\cdot a + (m+1-a)=m+1\\), which is not \\(b\\).  \nInstead, solve (1) for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{y-(m+1-i)}{i}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\((a,b)\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-(m+1-i)}{i}=a \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad b = i a + (m+1-i).\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\) exactly when \\(i=a\\). Since \\(a\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(m\\), there is a unique index \\(i\\) with this property, and consequently \\((a,b)\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (non‑sunny vertical covering)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ncover every point of \\(T_n\\). Hence the case \\(k=0\\) is feasible.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\) from the family (2). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main construction\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed and let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nk=n-s\\qquad (0\\le s\\le n).\n\\]\n\nWe shall produce \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n1. **Non‑sunny part.**  \n   Take the \\(s\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=s\\) from (2).  \n   These lines are not sunny and they cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(s\\).\n\n2. **Remaining points.**  \n   The set of points not yet covered is  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{n-s}'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge s+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Define the translation \\(\\tau:(x,y)\\mapsto (x-s,y)\\).  \n   Then \\(\\tau\\) maps \\(T_{n-s}'\\) bijectively onto \\(T_{n-s}\\).\n\n3. **Sunny covering of the reduced triangle.**  \n   By Lemma 1 applied with \\(m=n-s\\) there exist \\(n-s\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{\\,n-s}\\) that cover \\(T_{n-s}\\).  \n   Translating each of these lines back by the vector \\((s,0)\\) (i.e. applying \\(\\tau^{-1}\\)) yields \\(n-s\\) sunny lines that cover exactly the points of \\(T_{n-s}'\\).\n\n4. **Assembling the whole family.**  \n   The union of the \\(s\\) vertical lines and the \\(n-s\\) translated sunny lines consists of \\(s+(n-s)=n\\) distinct lines.  \n   By construction every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them, and precisely \\(n-s=k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nSince the integer \\(s\\) can be any value between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realized.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
