 distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a_1\\neq a_2\\).\nTheir slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a_i,b_i\\) are positive integers and \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), the numerator and denominator are integers whose absolute values are at most \\(n\\).  \nIf the slope were \\(0\\) we would have \\(b_1=b_2\\); then \\(a_1+b_1\\le n+1\\) and \\(a_2+b_2\\le n+1\\) imply \\(a_1\\neq a_2\\) but the same \\(b\\), which is impossible because the points would lie on a horizontal line, contradicting the definition of a sunny line.  \nSimilarly a vertical slope would require \\(a_1=a_2\\), which cannot happen for two distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with different abscissae.  \nFinally, a slope \\(-1\\) would give \\(b_2-b_1=-(a_2-a_1)\\), i.e. \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2\\). Since all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and the equality can hold for many pairs, the line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain several points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nConsequently **any line whose slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) can contain at most one point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)**.  \nIf we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), at most one point can be assigned to each sunny line, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of points, which is \\(T_n\\).  \nBecause we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, the sharper bound is  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\)\n\n#### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nUse the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) has \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence it lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny.  \nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n#### 2.2  Adding sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe replace the first \\(k\\) vertical lines by \\(k\\) sunny lines, each passing through a distinct point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and through no other point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac{1}{i}\\,x+\\Bigl(n+1-i-\\frac{1}{i}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Verification that \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.*  \nIts slope is \\(\\frac{1}{i}\\), which is a positive rational number different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*Verification that \\((i,n+1-i)\\in\\ell_i\\).*  \nSubstituting \\(x=i\\) into (2) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{1}{i}\\,i+n+1-i-\\frac{1}{i}=n+1-i,\n\\]\n\nso the point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  \nBecause \\(i+(n+1-i)=n+1\\), this point belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Uniqueness of the integer point on \\(\\ell_i\\).*  \nAssume \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) also satisfies (2). Multiplying (2) by \\(i\\) yields  \n\n\\[\ni b = a + i\\bigl(n+1-i\\bigr)-1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand side of (3) is congruent to \\(-1\\pmod{i}\\), whereas the left‑hand side is a multiple of \\(i\\). Hence (3) cannot hold for any integer \\(a\\) unless the term \\(-1\\) is cancelled, which occurs precisely when \\(a=i\\) and consequently \\(b=n+1-i\\). Therefore \\(\\ell_i\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) **only** at \\((i,n+1-i)\\).\n\nNow keep the remaining \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=k+1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nThese lines are non‑sunny and, together with the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\), form a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Covering property.*  \n- The points \\((i,n+1-i)\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) are covered by the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\).  \n- Any other point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) has \\(a\\ge k+1\\); thus it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* From the geometric observation in §1, any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n\\).  \n* In §2 we produced, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
