n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of required points  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each abscissa \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\}= \\{(a,1),(a,2),\\dots ,(a,n+1-a)\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\).  In particular  \n\n\\[\n|C_a|\\ge 2\\quad\\text{iff}\\quad a\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of points is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemma\u202f1 (intersection with a column)\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> Let \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is **not** vertical. Then for every \\(a\\) the line \\(\\ell\\) contains at most one point of the column \\(C_a\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011vertical line has an equation \\(y=mx+c\\) with a finite slope \\(m\\).  \nIf \\((a,b_1)\\) and \\((a,b_2)\\) with \\(b_1\\neq b_2\\) were both on \\(\\ell\\), substituting \\(x=a\\) would give\n\\(b_1=ma+c=b_2\\), a contradiction. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A lower bound on the number of vertical lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nv:=|\\{\\,\\ell\\in\\mathcal F\\mid \\ell\\text{ is vertical}\\,\\}|,\\qquad\ns:=n-v\n\\]\n\nso that \\(s\\) is the number of non\u2011vertical lines in \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nFor each column \\(C_a\\) we count how many of its points are covered by the non\u2011vertical lines.\nBy Lemma\u202f1 each non\u2011vertical line contributes **at most one** point to \\(C_a\\); consequently the total number of points of \\(C_a\\) that can be covered by the \\(s\\) non\u2011vertical lines is at most \\(s\\).\n\nHence, for every \\(a\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{points of }C_a\\text{ covered by vertical lines}\\;\\ge\\;|C_a|-s .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA vertical line can cover **all** points of a column, but a single vertical line can belong to at most one column. Therefore the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(v\\) vertical lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{a\\in V}|C_a|,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(V\\subset\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is the set of abscissae of the vertical lines. Since each vertical line corresponds to a distinct \\(a\\), we have \\(|V|=v\\).\n\nNow sum inequality (4) over all columns \\(a=1,\\dots ,n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{a=1}^{n}\\bigl(|C_a|-s\\bigr)\n\\;\\le\\;\n\\text{total points covered by vertical lines}\n\\;\\le\\;\n\\sum_{a\\in V}|C_a|.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe left\u2011hand side equals  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl(\\sum_{a=1}^{n}|C_a|\\Bigr)-ns\n=|\\mathcal P_n|-ns\n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-ns .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe right\u2011hand side is the sum of the sizes of exactly \\(v\\) columns, each size being at most \\(|C_1|=n\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{a\\in V}|C_a|\\le vn .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nCombining (5)\u2013(7) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-ns\\;\\le\\; vn .\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(s=n-v\\); substituting gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n(n-v)\\;\\le\\; vn\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n^2+nv\\;\\le\\; vn .\n\\]\n\nCancelling the term \\(nv\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n^2\\;\\le\\;0\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n-\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\le 0,\n\\]\n\nwhich is always true.  To obtain a **non\u2011trivial** inequality we sharpen the estimate (7): among the \\(v\\) chosen columns we can certainly include the \\(v\\) largest ones, namely \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_v\\), whose sizes are \\(n,\\,n-1,\\dots ,n+1-v\\). Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{a\\in V}|C_a|\\le n+(n-1)+\\dots +(n+1-v)\n= v n-\\frac{v(v-1)}{2}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nInsert (8) into (5)\u2013(6):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-ns\\;\\le\\; v n-\\frac{v(v-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nReplace \\(s=n-v\\) and simplify:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n(n-v)\\;\\le\\; vn-\\frac{v(v-1)}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n^2+nv\\;\\le\\; vn-\\frac{v(v-1)}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}+nv\\;\\le\\; vn-\\frac{v(v-1)}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\;\\le\\; -\\frac{v(v-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(-2\\) (which reverses the inequality) gives  \n\n\\[\nn(n-1)\\;\\ge\\; v(v-1).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince both sides are non\u2011negative, (9) implies \\(v\\ge n-1\\).  \nIndeed, the quadratic function \\(x\\mapsto x(x-1)\\) is strictly increasing for \\(x\\ge 1\\); because \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)(n-2)=n^2-3n+2 < n^2-n = n(n-1),\n\\]\n\nso the largest integer \\(v\\) satisfying (9) is \\(v=n\\) or \\(v=n-1\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,v\\ge n-1\\,}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus any covering family of \\(n\\) lines must contain at least \\(n-1\\) vertical lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nA vertical line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence it is **not** sunny.  \nFrom (10) we have at most one line that is not vertical; therefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 1 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Constructions showing that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable  \n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n  \\]\n\n  For any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), so the point lies on \\(L_a\\).  \n  All lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none is sunny.  This yields a covering with \\(k=0\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with a single sunny line passing through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\).  \n  Choose, for example,\n\n  \\[\n  L_{\\text{sun}}:\\; y = 2(x-n)+1 = 2x-2n+1 .\n  \\]\n\n  The slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); thus \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\) is sunny.  \n  The vertical lines cover every column \\(C_a\\) for \\(a\\le n-1\\) completely, and \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\) covers the lone point \\((n,1)\\).  \n  Hence all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are covered and exactly one line is sunny, giving \\(k=1\\).\n\nBoth constructions satisfy the required covering condition, so the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are indeed realizable.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a covering family \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains at least two sunny lines.  \nSunny lines are non\u2011vertical, so they belong to the set of \\(s=n-v\\) non\u2011vertical lines.  \nHaving at least two sunny lines forces \\(s\\ge 2\\), i.e. \\(v\\le n-2\\).  \nThis contradicts the necessary inequality \\(v\\ge n-1\\) proved in (10).  \nHence no covering family can have two or more sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Final answer\n\nThe analysis above shows that the covering requirement forces at least \\(n-1\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines, leaving at most one line that can be sunny.  Both possibilities \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are achievable, and \\(k\\ge 2\\) is impossible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\;}\n\\]"
